## Impact Section

## Business Problem

Estimating housing prices accurately is vital for real-estate firms, brokers and investors to make informed decisions. This project aims to identify key drivers of price variation and build a regression model to predict market values.

## Analytical Approach

Performed exploratory data analysis to uncover relationships between housing features (area, number of rooms, location, furnishing status) and the target variable (price).

Cleaned and pre-processed data: handled missing values, encoded categorical variables and removed outliers.

Built a linear regression model (using scikit-learn) to predict house prices, and evaluated it using metrics like R², MSE and RMSE.

Analyzed model coefficients to interpret feature importance and extract actionable insights.

## Key Insights

“Area” (square footage) and “number of rooms” showed the strongest linear association with price.

Furnished homes and those in prime locations commanded higher premiums — underscoring the importance of non-size features.

The model explained a large portion of variance in prices (e.g., R² ~ 0.80) and residuals exhibited approximately normal distribution, indicating good fit.

## Business Impact 

This regression framework equips stakeholders with a quantitative tool to forecast market values and identify which features add most value.

Enables quicker pricing decisions, improved investment analysis and better portfolio risk assessment for real-estate businesses.

Demonstrates end-to-end workflow—data preprocessing, modeling, interpretation—which highlights your capability to turn raw data into business insights.

